---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Terms of Service — Notare"
  description="The terms for using Notare — free, open source, provided as is."
>
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Legal</p>
      <h1 class="section-title" style="max-width: none;">Terms of Service</h1>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Notare is a free, open-source app. These terms are short and
        plain-English on purpose — there's nothing to sign up for and nothing to
        pay for the core app.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: 21 July 2026</p>

      <div class="legal">
        <h2>1. Acceptance</h2>
        <p>
          By downloading, installing, or using Notare, you agree to these terms.
          If you don't agree, please don't use the app.
        </p>

        <h2>2. License &amp; cost</h2>
        <p>
          Notare is free and open source, released under the
          <strong>MIT License</strong>. The full source code is available at
          <a href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare">github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare</a>.
          You may use, modify, distribute, and self-host Notare in accordance
          with the terms of the MIT License.
        </p>

        <h2>3. "As is", no warranty</h2>
        <p>
          Notare is provided <strong>"as is"</strong>, without warranties of any
          kind, express or implied — including, without limitation, the implied
          warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, and
          non-infringement. This is consistent with the MIT License. You use
          Notare at your own risk.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Notare+ and premium features</h2>
        <p>
          The core app is free. Some optional, clearly-marked premium or hosted
          features may be offered separately (for example, a hosted
          transcription or companion service). If you choose to use any such
          paid feature, it is subject to any additional terms presented to you
          at the point of purchase. You are never required to use a paid feature
          to use Notare.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Your responsibilities</h2>
        <p>
          You are responsible for how you use Notare and for complying with all
          applicable laws. In particular,
          <strong>
            recording and transcribing conversations is subject to consent and
            recording laws that vary by jurisdiction
          </strong>
          — you are responsible for obtaining any consent required from meeting
          participants before recording.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Third-party services</h2>
        <p>
          Notare can connect to third-party services that you choose to
          configure — for example, Google Calendar, external LLM providers, or a
          self-hosted transcription server. Your use of those services is
          governed by their own terms and privacy policies. Notare is not
          responsible for third-party services or for how they handle your data.
        </p>

        <h2>7. Limitation of liability</h2>
        <p>
          To the maximum extent permitted by law, the authors and contributors
          of Notare are not liable for any indirect, incidental, special, or
          consequential damages, or for any loss of data, arising from your use
          of — or inability to use — Notare.
        </p>

        <h2>8. Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          Your use of Notare is also governed by our
          <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>9. Changes to these terms</h2>
        <p>
          These terms may be updated from time to time. The "Last updated" date
          above reflects the current version. Continued use of Notare after
          changes take effect constitutes acceptance of the revised terms.
        </p>

        <h2>10. Governing law</h2>
        <p>
          These terms are governed by the laws of India, without regard to
          conflict-of-laws rules.
        </p>

        <h2>11. Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about these terms? Reach out via the project's GitHub
          repository:
          <a href="https://github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare">github.com/abhi-wan-kenobi/notare</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .updated {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, Consolas,
      monospace;
  }

  .legal {
    margin-top: 40px;
    max-width: 70ch;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .legal h2 {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 640;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-top: 40px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .legal h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .legal h3 {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    font-weight: 620;
    margin-top: 26px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .legal p {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .legal p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .legal strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 620;
  }

  .legal ul {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    padding-left: 22px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .legal li {
    padding-left: 4px;
  }

  .legal li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .legal a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(77, 92, 255, 0.4);
  }

  .legal a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
